<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gournay-sur-Aronde</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th–1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gournay-sur-Aronde is a village in the Oise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the site of an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> Gallic sanctuary excavated from 1977 by Jean-Louis Brunaux. The enclosed sanctuary, in the territory of the Bellovaci, held deposits of thousands of deliberately broken weapons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and other animal remains and human bones. It became a reference site for understanding Gallic religious practice and warrior offerings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gournay-sur-aronde.jpg" alt="Gournay-sur-Aronde" width="320" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: dhenry_fr · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monument_fun%C3%A9raire_de_Madame_Jarry_de_Nancy_2013-09-06_20-58-34.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
